This document provides (i) the variables and parameters used for calibration and
uncertainty analysis, (ii) the per-reach calibration data, and (iii) the technical
implementation detail needed to reproduce or extend the engine: source-file
inventory, governing equations and solver, input/output file formats, build and
activation procedure, and the cross-validation harness. Section and table numbers
are prefixed ``S''. It accompanies the main text and uses the same notation.

% =====================================================================
\section{Parameters and variables}\label{si:params}

\subsection{PFAS physico-chemical and sorption parameters}
Table~\ref{tab:si-phys} lists the compound and sorption parameters the engine
reads. The global compound record (\texttt{pfas.dat}) carries the molecular
weight, aqueous solubility, the Langmuir air--water-interface coefficients, and the
runoff/percolation partition; the per-HRU initialization (\texttt{pfas\_hru.ini})
carries the initial soil pool and the Freundlich coefficients, assigned by soil
class and land use. Values shown are the PFOS defaults used in this study.

\begin{table}[ht]\centering\footnotesize
\caption{PFAS fate parameters read by the engine, with the PFOS values used here.
$C_w$ is the aqueous concentration; symbols match Eq.~(1) of the main text.}
\label{tab:si-phys}
\begin{tabular}{lllr}
\hline
Symbol & Description & Source / file & PFOS value \\
\hline
$M_w$            & molecular weight (kg\,mol\textsuperscript{-1})        & \texttt{pfas.dat} & 0.50013 \\
$S$              & aqueous solubility (mg\,L\textsuperscript{-1})        & \texttt{pfas.dat} & 680 \\
$K_L$            & Langmuir AWI affinity (L\,$\mu$mol\textsuperscript{-1})& \texttt{pfas.dat} & 0.137 \\
$\Gamma_{\max}$  & Langmuir AWI max surface excess ($\mu$mol\,m\textsuperscript{-2}) & \texttt{pfas.dat} & 2500 \\
$f_{\text{perc}}$& runoff/percolation partition (--)                    & \texttt{pfas.dat} & 0.20 \\
$\text{sol\_pfas}$ & initial soil PFOS pool ($\mu$g\,ha\textsuperscript{-1}) & \texttt{pfas\_hru.ini} & per HRU \\
$k_f$            & Freundlich coefficient ((nmol\,kg\textsuperscript{-1})/(nM)$^{n}$) & \texttt{pfas\_hru.ini} & 216--450 \\
$n$              & Freundlich exponent (--)                              & \texttt{pfas\_hru.ini} & 0.33--0.48 \\
$\text{enr}$     & sediment enrichment ratio (--)                        & \texttt{pfas\_hru.ini} & per HRU \\
$A_{aw}$         & air--water interfacial area (m\textsuperscript{2}\,m\textsuperscript{-3}) & from $\theta$, $d_{50}$ & derived \\
$k_d$            & in-stream sediment--water partition (L\,kg\textsuperscript{-1}) & $K_{oc}\cdot f_{oc}$ & derived \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Calibration parameters}
Calibration adjusts two global multipliers in \texttt{pfas\_calib.dat}: a
soil-loading scale on every HRU's initial soil pool and a multiplier on the
in-stream organic-carbon partition. Because in-stream concentration is, to first
order, linear in the soil-loading scale, the magnitude calibration is nearly
analytic (one forward evaluation fixes the scale to first order). The calibrated
values are soil-loading $=0.11$ and $K_{oc}$ multiplier $=1.0$.

\subsection{Uncertainty-analysis design}
Table~\ref{tab:si-ua} gives the five-parameter Latin-hypercube design (120 members,
uniform priors), the calibrated value of each parameter, and its Spearman rank
correlation with the reach-mean in-stream PFOS concentration across the ensemble.
The soil-loading multiplier dominates the response ($\rho=0.95$); the remaining
parameters are second-order or negligible, consistent with the near-linear
structure of the problem.

\begin{table}[ht]\centering\footnotesize
\caption{Uncertainty-analysis parameter design: prior ranges, calibrated values,
and Spearman rank correlation with reach-mean in-stream PFOS ($n=120$ members).}
\label{tab:si-ua}
